      "text": "To find the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\), we first need to determine the parametric equations of the line. Let's denote the vector on the line by \\( \\mathbf{r}(t) = \\begin{pmatrix} x(t) \\\\ y(t) \\end{pmatrix} \\).\n\nGiven:\n- At \\( t = 2 \\), the vector is \\( \\mathbf{r}(2) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} \\).\n- At \\( t = 3 \\), the vector is \\( \\mathbf{r}(3) = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} \\).\n\nLet's find the direction vector of the line:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(3) - \\mathbf{r}(2) = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe direction vector is \\( \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} \\). This can be written as \\( 2t \\mathbf{i} - 8t \\mathbf{j} \\) where \\( t = 3 - 2 = 1 \\) at \\( t = 2 \\) and \\( t = 3 - 2 = 1 \\) at \\( t = 3 \\).\n\nSo the parametric equations of the line are:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx(t) = 1 + 2(s - 2) = 1 + 2s - 4 = 2s - 3 \\\\\ny(t) = 4 - 8(s - 2) = 4 - 8s + 16 = -8s + 20\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nNow we need to find the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\):\n\\[\nx(-7) = 2(-7) - 3 = -14 - 3 = -17\n\\]\n\\[\ny(-7) = -8(-7) + 20 = 56 + 20 = 76\n\\]\n\nSo the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\) is:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -17 \\\\ 76 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -17 \\\\ 76 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
