      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the equation of the line parameterized by \\( t \\). We are given two points on the line at specific values of \\( t \\):\n\n- At \\( t = 2 \\), the vector is \\( \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} \\).\n- At \\( t = 3 \\), the vector is \\( \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} \\).\n\nThe general form of a parameterized line equation is given by:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\mathbf{r}_0 + t \\mathbf{d}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{r}_0\\) is a point on the line, \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is the direction vector, and \\( t \\) is a parameter.\n\nFirst, we need to determine the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\). The direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is the difference between the vectors at \\( t = 3 \\) and \\( t = 2 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{d} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - 1 \\\\ -4 - 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSo, the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nNext, we need to find the point \\(\\mathbf{r}_0\\) on the line. We can use the information at \\( t = 2 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(2) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the point \\(\\mathbf{r}_0 = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nNow we can write the parameterized equation of the line:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTo find the vector at \\( t = -7 \\), we substitute \\( t = -7 \\) into the equation:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(-7) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + (-7) \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nCalculate the vector multiplication:\n\\[\n-7 \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\cdot 2 \\\\ -7 \\cdot -8 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -14 \\\\ 56 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nAdd this to the point \\(\\mathbf{r}_0\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(-7) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -14 \\\\ 56 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 - 14 \\\\ 4 + 56 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
